export const E6={title:'Cardiopulmonary diseases I: obstructive lung diseases',hook:'Asthma affects about 1 in 12 Americans, and COPD is one of the leading causes of death in the United States. Both make it hard to get air out of the lungs. Cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis add thick mucus and repeated infections. Respiratory therapists treat these patients in clinics, emergency rooms, ICUs and homes. Cardiopulmonary diseases are 20% of the test.',objectives:['Explain the causes, signs and treatment of asthma, including severe exacerbations.','Describe emphysema and chronic bronchitis and the management of COPD and its exacerbations.','Describe cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis and their airway-clearance needs.','Compare obstructive diseases by history, exam and testing.'],sections:[
['Asthma',[
'**Asthma** is chronic airway **inflammation** with **bronchospasm**, swelling and mucus, causing **reversible** airflow obstruction. Triggers include allergens (dust mites, pollen, pets, mold), smoke, cold air, exercise, respiratory infections, NSAIDs or aspirin in some people and stress. Symptoms are episodic **wheezing**, cough (often at night), chest tightness and shortness of breath. Diagnosis uses history and **spirometry** showing reversible obstruction; **peak flow** monitoring and **FeNO** can help.',
'Treatment is stepwise: **inhaled corticosteroids** are the core controller; **short-acting beta₂ agonists** (albuterol) relieve symptoms; **ICS–formoterol** can serve as both controller and reliever in some plans; **LABAs** are always combined with an ICS; leukotriene modifiers and **biologic** injections help selected patients. A **severe exacerbation** shows accessory muscle use, inability to speak in sentences, SpO₂ below about 90% and a falling peak flow; treat with repeated bronchodilators (albuterol, often with **ipratropium**), **systemic corticosteroids**, oxygen and close monitoring. A **“silent chest”**—little air movement with no wheeze—and a **rising or normal PaCO₂** in a struggling patient signal impending respiratory failure.',
sort('Controller or rescue?',['Long-term controller','Quick-relief (rescue)'],[['Inhaled corticosteroid',0],['Albuterol',1],['Montelukast',0],['Ipratropium in the emergency department',1],['ICS plus LABA combination',0],['Levalbuterol',1]])]],
['COPD: emphysema and chronic bronchitis',[
'**COPD** is persistent, progressive airflow limitation, most often from **cigarette smoking**; other causes include occupational dusts, indoor biomass smoke and **alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency** (consider in young patients or nonsmokers with emphysema). **Emphysema** destroys alveolar walls, reducing elastic recoil and surface area; patients are often thin, use **pursed-lip breathing** and have a **barrel chest** and hyperinflation. **Chronic bronchitis** is a productive cough for at least **3 months in each of 2 consecutive years**, with mucus-gland enlargement; patients may have cyanosis, edema and **cor pulmonale** (right-heart failure from lung disease).',
'Diagnosis requires spirometry with a **post-bronchodilator FEV₁/FVC below 0.70**. Management follows the **GOLD** strategy: **smoking cessation** (the only intervention proven to slow decline besides oxygen for severe hypoxemia), long-acting bronchodilators (**LAMA**, **LABA** or both), ICS for frequent exacerbations with eosinophilia, vaccines, **pulmonary rehabilitation** and **long-term oxygen** for resting hypoxemia (PaO₂ ≤55 mm Hg or SpO₂ ≤88%). Exacerbations—worse dyspnea, more or purulent sputum—are treated with bronchodilators, systemic steroids, antibiotics when indicated, controlled oxygen (often target **88–92%**) and **noninvasive ventilation (BiPAP)** for hypercapnic respiratory failure.',
sort('Emphysema or chronic bronchitis?',['More typical of emphysema','More typical of chronic bronchitis'],[['Productive cough 3 months a year for 2 years',1],['Destroyed alveolar walls and loss of recoil',0],['Thin with pursed-lip breathing',0],['Edema and cor pulmonale',1],['Low DLCO',0],['Mucus gland enlargement',1]]),
label('resp','Where obstructive diseases strike',[['bronchi','Bronchi','Inflamed and narrowed in asthma; dilated in bronchiectasis.'],['bronchioles','Bronchioles','Small airways obstructed in COPD.'],['alveoli','Alveoli','Walls destroyed in emphysema.'],['diaphragm','Diaphragm','Flattened by hyperinflation.'],['trachea','Trachea','Large airway; stridor if obstructed above it.']])]],
['Cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis',[
'**Cystic fibrosis (CF)** is an **autosomal recessive** disease caused by mutations in the **CFTR** gene, which controls chloride and water movement across cell membranes. Thick, sticky mucus clogs the airways, pancreas and intestines, causing chronic cough, repeated lung infections (*Pseudomonas aeruginosa*, *Staphylococcus aureus*), **digital clubbing**, poor growth, fatty stools and salty skin. Newborn screening and the **sweat chloride test** diagnose it.',
'Treatment includes daily **airway clearance** (chest physiotherapy, high-frequency chest wall oscillation vests, positive expiratory pressure devices, exercise), inhaled **dornase alfa** and **hypertonic saline** to thin mucus, inhaled antibiotics, pancreatic enzymes and nutrition, and **CFTR modulator** drugs that correct the protein defect in many patients. **Bronchiectasis** is permanent widening of the bronchi from repeated infection or inflammation, causing chronic productive cough with large amounts of sputum; it is treated with airway clearance and antibiotics for exacerbations.',
match('CF and bronchiectasis',[['CFTR gene','Mutated in cystic fibrosis'],['Sweat chloride test','Diagnoses cystic fibrosis'],['Dornase alfa','Breaks down DNA in thick mucus'],['Hypertonic saline','Draws water into mucus'],['Chest wall oscillation vest','Airway clearance device'],['Bronchiectasis','Permanently widened bronchi with heavy sputum']])]],
['Comparing obstructive diseases',[
'All obstructive diseases reduce the **FEV₁/FVC ratio**, but their stories differ. **Asthma** usually starts in childhood, comes and goes, has allergic features and reverses with bronchodilators. **COPD** usually affects smokers over 40 and progresses slowly with incomplete reversibility. **CF** begins in infancy with growth problems and frequent infections. **Bronchiectasis** produces large volumes of sputum daily. Some patients have features of both asthma and COPD.',
"Testing helps separate them: **eosinophil counts** and **FeNO** point to allergic (type 2) inflammation that responds to inhaled steroids and biologics; **alpha-1 antitrypsin levels** screen for inherited emphysema; and **chest CT** shows emphysema or dilated bronchi. Knowing the pattern lets the team match therapy to the disease.",
'Respiratory therapists teach **inhaler technique**, check adherence, create **action plans** (green, yellow and red zones) and encourage vaccination and smoking cessation. Many readmissions for COPD and asthma can be prevented with better self-management, follow-up within days of discharge and pulmonary rehabilitation.',
flip('Quick comparison',[['Asthma','Childhood onset, triggers, reversible'],['COPD','Smoker over 40, progressive, not fully reversible'],['Cystic fibrosis','Genetic; thick mucus; infancy'],['Bronchiectasis','Dilated airways; large daily sputum'],['Silent chest','Dangerously little air movement'],['BiPAP','Noninvasive ventilation for hypercapnic COPD exacerbation']])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Severe asthma',{a:{text:'A 16-year-old with asthma arrives speaking in single words, using accessory muscles, SpO₂ 88%. Wheezing was loud an hour ago, but now you hear very little air movement. (Scenario invented for practice.) What does the quiet chest mean?',choices:[['Severe obstruction with impending respiratory failure—treat urgently','b','Correct. A silent chest is ominous.',true],['The asthma is improving','b','Little air movement means worse, not better.',false]]},b:{text:'Which treatments are appropriate per orders?',choices:[['Oxygen, continuous or repeated albuterol with ipratropium, systemic steroids and close monitoring','c','Correct. Aggressive bronchodilation and steroids.',true],['A single puff of an inhaled steroid and discharge','c','This is a life-threatening exacerbation.',false]]},c:{text:'You monitor SpO₂, breath sounds, work of breathing and a blood gas; the team prepares for escalation if CO₂ rises.',end:true}}),
chk('Which long-term therapy has been shown to improve survival in COPD with severe resting hypoxemia?',['Long-term oxygen therapy','Daily antibiotics for everyone','Bed rest','Cough suppressants'],'Plus smoking cessation.')]],
["Exacerbation prevention and the RT’s role",["Most obstructive-disease hospitalizations are triggered by **viral infections**, **air pollution**, **allergen exposure**, **missed medicines** and **poor inhaler technique**. RTs check technique at every visit, because many patients use inhalers incorrectly; review whether controller medicines are taken daily; and confirm that patients have a working rescue inhaler and an up-to-date action plan. Vaccines for influenza, pneumococcus, COVID-19 and RSV (for eligible older adults) reduce exacerbations.","After discharge for a COPD or asthma exacerbation, a follow-up visit within about a week, referral to **pulmonary rehabilitation** and smoking cessation support lower readmission risk. In the hospital, RTs use **protocols** to adjust bronchodilator frequency based on assessment, wean oxygen to target and transition patients from nebulizers to their home inhalers before discharge.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Raises or lowers exacerbation risk?","buckets":["Raises risk","Lowers risk"],"items":[["Skipping the daily inhaled steroid",0],["Annual influenza vaccine",1],["Smoking",0],["Correct spacer technique",1],["Pulmonary rehabilitation",1],["No written action plan",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Asthma: inflammation and reversible bronchospasm; ICS controllers, SABA rescue, LABA only with ICS; severe attacks need bronchodilators, systemic steroids and oxygen; a silent chest or rising CO₂ is ominous.','COPD (emphysema and chronic bronchitis): smoking-related, FEV₁/FVC <0.70 after bronchodilator; smoking cessation, LAMA/LABA, rehab, vaccines, long-term oxygen for severe hypoxemia; exacerbations may need BiPAP and target SpO₂ 88–92%.','Cystic fibrosis (CFTR, sweat chloride, airway clearance, dornase alfa, modulators) and bronchiectasis (dilated airways, heavy sputum).'],terms:[['Bronchospasm','Sudden tightening of airway smooth muscle.'],['Cor pulmonale','Right-heart failure caused by lung disease.'],['Bronchiectasis','Permanent abnormal widening of the bronchi.']],mcqs:[q('Asthma obstruction is typically:','Reversible','Permanent','Restrictive','Absent between attacks only in adults','Improves with bronchodilators.'),q('Chronic bronchitis is a productive cough for at least:','3 months in each of 2 consecutive years','1 week','10 days','6 months once','Clinical definition.'),q('Which inherited condition can cause emphysema in young nonsmokers?','Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency','Cystic fibrosis only','Down syndrome','Hemophilia','Consider testing.'),q('Cystic fibrosis is diagnosed with:','The sweat chloride test','A chest X-ray alone','A peak flow meter','A throat swab','Plus genetic testing.'),q('A “silent chest” in a severe asthma attack means:','Very little air movement—an emergency','Recovery','Normal breathing','A pneumothorax only','Ominous sign.'),q('What target SpO₂ is often ordered for COPD patients with CO₂ retention?','88–92%','100%','75–80%','95–100% always','Controlled oxygen.'),q('Which therapy breaks down DNA in CF mucus?','Dornase alfa','Albuterol','Prednisone','Montelukast','Thins secretions.'),q('Noninvasive ventilation is especially useful for:','Hypercapnic COPD exacerbation','Mild seasonal allergies','Stable asthma','Pneumonia prevention only','BiPAP reduces intubation.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare asthma and COPD by cause, typical patient, spirometry and long-term treatment, and describe how a respiratory therapist would manage an acute COPD exacerbation.',rubric:[['Cause and patient',['inflammation','allergic','childhood','smoking','over 40','progressive']],['Spirometry',['reversible','12%','fev1/fvc','0.70','not fully reversible']],['Long-term treatment',['inhaled corticosteroid','saba','laba','lama','smoking cessation','rehab','vaccines','oxygen']],['Exacerbation care',['bronchodilator','ipratropium','steroids','antibiotics','88-92','abg','bipap','monitor']]],model:'Asthma is chronic airway inflammation with reversible bronchospasm, often beginning in childhood with allergic triggers, while COPD is progressive airflow limitation usually caused by smoking in adults over 40. On spirometry, asthma shows obstruction that improves significantly—about 12% and 200 mL—after a bronchodilator, whereas COPD shows a post-bronchodilator FEV₁/FVC below 0.70 that does not fully reverse. Long-term asthma treatment centers on inhaled corticosteroids with a rescue inhaler, adding a LABA only together with an ICS. COPD treatment centers on smoking cessation, long-acting bronchodilators (LAMA and LABA), vaccines, pulmonary rehabilitation and long-term oxygen for severe resting hypoxemia, with an ICS for some patients with frequent exacerbations. For an acute COPD exacerbation, I would assess work of breathing, breath sounds, vital signs and SpO₂, give short-acting bronchodilators such as albuterol with ipratropium by nebulizer or MDI with a spacer, and support the team’s plan for systemic corticosteroids and antibiotics when sputum is purulent. I would titrate controlled oxygen to a target such as 88–92%, obtain an arterial blood gas to check for rising CO₂ and, if the patient has hypercapnic respiratory failure, set up BiPAP to reduce the need for intubation, monitoring the patient closely and documenting the response.'},sources:[RULES,NHLBI_ASTHMA,NHLBI_COPD,NHLBI_CF]};

export const E7={title:'Cardiopulmonary diseases II: infections, restrictive and vascular disorders',hook:'Pneumonia is a leading cause of hospitalization; ARDS can turn a healthy person critically ill in a day; a pulmonary embolism can kill within minutes. Respiratory therapists help recognize these conditions early and provide oxygen, ventilation and airway care. This lesson completes the cardiopulmonary diseases section.',objectives:['Describe pneumonia, tuberculosis and other respiratory infections.','Explain ARDS, pulmonary edema, pneumothorax, pleural effusion and interstitial lung disease.','Recognize pulmonary embolism and pulmonary hypertension.','Describe lung cancer and obstructive sleep apnea and the RT’s role.'],sections:[
['Pneumonia and tuberculosis',[
'**Pneumonia** is infection of the lung parenchyma. **Community-acquired** pneumonia is often caused by *Streptococcus pneumoniae*, viruses, *Mycoplasma* or *Haemophilus influenzae*; **hospital-acquired** and **ventilator-associated** pneumonias involve more resistant organisms. Signs include **fever, productive cough, pleuritic pain, tachypnea, crackles, bronchial breath sounds and increased fremitus** over consolidation, with an infiltrate on chest X-ray. Treatment: antibiotics, oxygen, fluids, airway clearance and early mobility. **Aspiration pneumonia** follows inhaling oral or stomach contents—common in people with swallowing problems.',
'Prevention of **ventilator-associated pneumonia** includes **raising the head of the bed 30–45 degrees**, daily sedation interruption and breathing trials, oral care, subglottic suctioning and hand hygiene. **Tuberculosis** spreads through the air; **latent** TB has no symptoms, while **active** TB causes chronic cough, hemoptysis, fever, night sweats and weight loss and requires **airborne isolation** and multi-drug therapy. RTs collect **induced sputum** specimens in negative-pressure rooms while wearing N95 respirators.',
match('Infection facts',[['Most common bacterial cause of CAP','Streptococcus pneumoniae'],['Head of bed 30–45°','Prevents ventilator-associated pneumonia'],['Aspiration pneumonia','Inhaled oral or stomach contents'],['Active TB','Airborne isolation and multi-drug therapy'],['Bronchial breath sounds over a lobe','Consolidation'],['Induced sputum','Specimen collection for TB']])]],
['ARDS and pulmonary edema',[
'**Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)** is widespread lung inflammation with leaky capillaries that floods the alveoli, triggered by sepsis, pneumonia, aspiration, trauma, pancreatitis or transfusion. The **Berlin definition** requires onset within **1 week**, **bilateral opacities** on imaging, respiratory failure **not fully explained by heart failure** and a **PaO₂/FiO₂ ratio of 300 or less** with PEEP of at least 5 cm H₂O (mild ≤300, moderate ≤200, severe ≤100). Lungs become stiff (low compliance), and hypoxemia resists oxygen alone.',
'ARDS care uses **lung-protective ventilation**—**low tidal volumes of about 4–8 mL/kg of predicted body weight**, plateau pressure below 30 cm H₂O and adequate **PEEP**—plus **prone positioning** for severe cases, conservative fluids and treating the cause. **Cardiogenic pulmonary edema** comes from left-heart failure raising pressure in the lung capillaries; it causes orthopnea, crackles, pink frothy sputum and an enlarged heart on X-ray and is treated with diuretics, oxygen, **CPAP or BiPAP** and heart failure therapy.',
{t:'example',title:'Calculate the P/F ratio',prompt:'A ventilated patient has a PaO₂ of 75 mm Hg on an FiO₂ of 0.50 with PEEP 10. (Values invented for practice.)',steps:['P/F ratio = PaO₂ ÷ FiO₂','75 ÷ 0.50 = 150','150 is ≤200 but >100'],answer:'P/F ratio 150: moderate ARDS by the Berlin definition (if other criteria are met).'},
sort('ARDS or cardiogenic pulmonary edema?',['More typical of ARDS','More typical of cardiogenic edema'],[['Follows sepsis or pneumonia',0],['History of heart failure and orthopnea',1],['Normal heart size with bilateral opacities',0],['Improves quickly with diuretics',1],['Requires low tidal volume ventilation',0],['Elevated BNP and enlarged heart',1]])]],
['Pleural and interstitial disorders',[
'A **pneumothorax** is air in the pleural space that collapses part of the lung. **Spontaneous** pneumothorax can occur in tall, thin young people or with COPD; **traumatic** pneumothorax follows injury or procedures. Signs: sudden pleuritic pain, dyspnea, **absent breath sounds** and **hyperresonance** on the affected side. A **tension pneumothorax**—with hypotension, **tracheal deviation away** from the affected side and distended neck veins—is an emergency requiring **needle decompression** and a chest tube. A **pleural effusion** (fluid) causes dullness and decreased breath sounds and may be drained by **thoracentesis**.',
'**Interstitial lung diseases** (such as **idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis**, asbestosis, silicosis and sarcoidosis) scar the lung tissue, causing a **restrictive** pattern, dry cough, fine “velcro” crackles, clubbing and exertional hypoxemia. **Restrictive** problems also come from chest wall disorders (kyphoscoliosis, obesity) and neuromuscular diseases (ALS, muscular dystrophy, Guillain-Barré), where RTs monitor **vital capacity** and **negative inspiratory force** to detect respiratory failure.',
match('Pleural and interstitial terms',[['Tension pneumothorax','Tracheal deviation, hypotension—needle decompression'],['Pleural effusion','Fluid; dullness on percussion'],['Thoracentesis','Draining fluid from the pleural space'],['Pulmonary fibrosis','Restrictive scarring with velcro crackles'],['Negative inspiratory force','Measures breathing muscle strength'],['Chest tube','Removes air or fluid from the pleural space']])]],
['Pulmonary vascular disease',[
'A **pulmonary embolism (PE)** is a clot, usually from a leg **DVT**, that blocks pulmonary arteries. Risk factors follow **Virchow’s triad**—stasis (immobility, travel), vessel injury (surgery, trauma) and hypercoagulability (cancer, pregnancy, estrogen). Signs include **sudden dyspnea**, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxemia and sometimes hemoptysis or collapse; ABGs often show **respiratory alkalosis** with low PaO₂. **CT pulmonary angiography** confirms it; treatment is anticoagulation, oxygen and clot-busting therapy for massive PE.',
'**Pulmonary hypertension** is high pressure in the pulmonary arteries from causes such as chronic hypoxemia (COPD), left-heart disease, chronic clots or primary arterial disease. It strains the right ventricle and causes fatigue, dyspnea, edema and syncope; **oxygen** relieves hypoxic vasoconstriction, and specialized drugs (including inhaled nitric oxide in some settings) dilate pulmonary vessels.',
order('Clot pathway in PE',['Clot forms in a deep leg vein','Clot breaks free','Travels through the right heart','Lodges in a pulmonary artery','Creates dead space and low oxygen','Treat with anticoagulation and oxygen'])]],
['Lung cancer and sleep-disordered breathing',[
'**Lung cancer** is the leading cause of cancer death in the United States; **smoking** causes most cases, and radon, asbestos and air pollution add risk. **Non-small cell** lung cancer is most common; **small cell** lung cancer grows quickly and is strongly linked to smoking. Warning signs include persistent cough, hemoptysis, weight loss, hoarseness and recurrent pneumonia. Annual **low-dose CT** screening is recommended for adults **50–80** with a **20 pack-year** history who smoke or quit within 15 years. RTs support patients with oxygen, airway clearance, breathing techniques and palliative care.',
'**Obstructive sleep apnea** is repeated collapse of the upper airway during sleep, causing snoring, apneas, oxygen dips and daytime sleepiness; it is diagnosed by **polysomnography** and treated with **CPAP**, which RTs often fit and teach. **Central sleep apnea**—pauses without breathing effort—occurs with heart failure, stroke and opioid use.',
"For all of these conditions, RTs titrate oxygen, support ventilation and monitor closely.",
sort('Match the condition',['Pulmonary embolism','Lung cancer','Obstructive sleep apnea'],[['Sudden dyspnea after surgery',0],['Weight loss and hemoptysis in a long-time smoker',1],['Loud snoring and witnessed apneas',2],['Diagnosed by CT pulmonary angiography',0],['Screened by low-dose CT',1],['Treated with CPAP',2]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Sudden deterioration',{a:{text:'A ventilated trauma patient suddenly has rising airway pressures, falling SpO₂ and blood pressure, absent breath sounds on the right and tracheal deviation to the left. What is happening? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Right tension pneumothorax','b','Correct. Absent sounds, deviation away and hypotension.',true],['Mucus plug in the left lung','b','Tracheal deviation and shock point to tension pneumothorax.',false]]},b:{text:'What is the immediate treatment?',choices:[['Alert the team for needle decompression and chest tube placement while giving 100% oxygen','c','Correct. It is a life-threatening emergency.',true],['Order a routine chest X-ray for tomorrow','c','Do not delay treatment.',false]]},c:{text:'After decompression, breath sounds return and blood pressure improves. You document the event and ventilator changes.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Pneumonia: fever, productive cough, crackles, consolidation; VAP prevention (head of bed 30–45°, oral care, sedation breaks); active TB requires airborne isolation.','ARDS: within 1 week, bilateral opacities, not cardiac, P/F ≤300 with PEEP ≥5; low tidal volume 4–8 mL/kg PBW, PEEP, prone positioning; cardiogenic edema responds to diuretics and CPAP/BiPAP.','Tension pneumothorax (deviation away, hypotension—decompress), effusion (dullness, thoracentesis), fibrosis (restrictive), PE (sudden dyspnea, respiratory alkalosis, CT angiography, anticoagulation), lung cancer and OSA.'],terms:[['ARDS','Acute respiratory distress syndrome—widespread lung injury with leaky capillaries.'],['P/F ratio','PaO₂ divided by FiO₂, used to grade ARDS.'],['Tension pneumothorax','Air trapped under pressure in the chest that compresses the heart and lungs.']],mcqs:[q('A P/F ratio of 150 with other criteria met indicates:','Moderate ARDS','Mild ARDS','Severe ARDS','Normal lungs','≤200 and >100.'),q('Lung-protective ventilation for ARDS uses tidal volumes of about:','4–8 mL/kg predicted body weight','15 mL/kg','20 mL/kg','1 mL/kg','Low tidal volume.'),q('Which sign suggests tension pneumothorax?','Tracheal deviation away from the affected side','Dullness to percussion','Bilateral crackles','Stridor','With hypotension.'),q('Raising the head of the bed 30–45° helps prevent:','Ventilator-associated pneumonia','Pneumothorax','Asthma','Lung cancer','Reduces aspiration.'),q('A typical ABG in acute pulmonary embolism shows:','Respiratory alkalosis with low PaO₂','Metabolic alkalosis','Respiratory acidosis always','Normal values','From hyperventilation.'),q('Pulmonary fibrosis produces which pattern?','Restrictive','Obstructive','Normal','Reversible obstruction','Small lung volumes.'),q('Which test confirms pulmonary embolism?','CT pulmonary angiography','Sweat chloride','Peak flow','Spirometry','Shows the clot.'),q('Which is treated with CPAP that RTs often fit?','Obstructive sleep apnea','Pneumothorax','Pleural effusion','Lung cancer','Keeps the airway open.')],frq:{prompt:'A 45-year-old with severe pneumonia and sepsis develops worsening hypoxemia. Chest X-ray shows bilateral opacities, the echocardiogram is normal and her PaO₂ is 64 mm Hg on an FiO₂ of 0.80 with PEEP 8. Classify her condition and explain the respiratory therapist’s role in her management.',rubric:[['Diagnosis',['ards','berlin','bilateral','not cardiac','pneumonia','sepsis']],['Calculation',['p/f','80','64 ÷ 0.80','severe','≤100','moderate']],['Ventilator strategy',['low tidal volume','4-8','predicted body weight','plateau','30','peep']],['Additional care',['prone','oxygen','abg','vap','head of bed','monitor','sedation','document']]],model:'She has acute respiratory distress syndrome: the onset is within a week of a known trigger (pneumonia and sepsis), the chest X-ray shows bilateral opacities and the normal echocardiogram makes heart failure an unlikely explanation. Her P/F ratio is 64 ÷ 0.80 = 80, which is 100 or less with PEEP of at least 5, so this is severe ARDS. The respiratory therapist helps provide lung-protective ventilation: setting low tidal volumes of about 4–8 mL/kg of predicted body weight (often starting at 6), keeping the plateau pressure below 30 cm H₂O and titrating PEEP with FiO₂ according to protocol to improve oxygenation while avoiding overdistension. Because her ARDS is severe, the team would consider prone positioning for long sessions, and the RT secures the airway and monitors during turns. The RT also obtains and interprets arterial blood gases, monitors SpO₂, compliance and pressures, applies ventilator-associated pneumonia prevention measures such as raising the head of the bed 30–45 degrees and oral care, participates in daily sedation and spontaneous breathing assessments as she improves and documents every change and response.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_PNA,CDC_TB,NHLBI_ARDS,NHLBI_VTE]};

export const E8={title:'Oxygen therapy and basic airway management (Skills I and III)',hook:'Oxygen is the most common drug respiratory therapists give, and opening an airway is the most basic life-saving skill they perform. Too little oxygen damages the brain within minutes; too much can harm some patients. A blocked airway can kill faster than almost anything else. Skills I and III test both. Basic therapeutics is 20% of the test.',objectives:['Identify indications, hazards and goals of oxygen therapy.','Compare low-flow and high-flow oxygen devices and their FiO₂ ranges.','Administer oxygen by nasal cannula following HOSA Skill III.','Open an airway and ventilate with a bag-valve-mask following HOSA Skill I.'],sections:[
['Indications and hazards of oxygen',[
'Oxygen is a **prescribed drug**: the order states the device and flow or concentration, often with a target **SpO₂** range (commonly **92–96%** for most adults, **88–92%** for patients at risk of CO₂ retention). Indications include **hypoxemia** (low PaO₂ or SpO₂), suspected hypoxia in emergencies such as trauma, shock, carbon monoxide poisoning and heart attack with low saturation, and severe breathlessness with low oxygen. Signs of hypoxia include tachypnea, tachycardia, restlessness, confusion and, late, cyanosis.',
'Hazards include **fire**, **oxygen toxicity** from high concentrations over long periods, **absorption atelectasis** (nitrogen washout lets alveoli collapse), worsening **hypercapnia** in some COPD patients, and **retinopathy of prematurity** in premature infants. The goal is always the **lowest concentration that achieves the target**.',
sort('Indication or hazard?',['Indication for oxygen','Hazard of oxygen'],[['SpO₂ 86% on room air',0],['Absorption atelectasis',1],['Carbon monoxide poisoning',0],['Retinopathy of prematurity',1],['Fire risk near a flame',1],['Shock with poor perfusion',0]])]],
['Oxygen delivery devices',[
'**Low-flow** devices provide only part of the patient’s inspired gas, so the delivered FiO₂ varies with breathing pattern. A **nasal cannula** runs at **1–6 L/min**, roughly **24–44%** FiO₂ (about 4% more per liter above room air’s 21%). A **simple mask** runs at **5–10 L/min** (at least 5 to flush exhaled CO₂), about **35–50%**. A **nonrebreather mask** with a reservoir bag runs at **10–15 L/min** so the bag stays at least one-third full, delivering about **60–90%**—used in emergencies.',
'**High-flow** devices meet or exceed the patient’s inspiratory demand, delivering a **precise FiO₂**: an **air-entrainment (Venturi) mask** delivers set concentrations from about **24–50%**, ideal for COPD; a **high-flow nasal cannula** delivers heated, humidified gas at up to about **60 L/min** with FiO₂ up to 100%. **Humidifiers** are often added at higher flows to prevent drying; HOSA Skill III uses a cannula **without humidification**.',
{t:'table',caption:'Common oxygen devices',head:['Device','Flow','Approximate FiO₂'],rows:[['Nasal cannula','1–6 L/min','24–44%'],['Simple mask','5–10 L/min','35–50%'],['Nonrebreather','10–15 L/min','60–90%'],['Venturi mask','Set by adapter','24–50% (precise)'],['High-flow nasal cannula','Up to ~60 L/min','21–100%']]},
match('Device to use',[['Emergency with severe hypoxemia','Nonrebreather mask'],['COPD needing a precise 28%','Venturi mask'],['Stable patient needing 2 L/min','Nasal cannula'],['Very high flow with heated humidity','High-flow nasal cannula'],['Must run at least 5 L/min','Simple mask'],['Bag must stay one-third full','Nonrebreather']])]],
['Nasal cannula: Skill III',[
'Skill III has 19 steps in **6 minutes**. **1.** Obtain and review the **physician’s order**. **2.** Obtain equipment. **3.** Hand hygiene and gloves. **4.** Greet and identify the patient with **two identifiers**. **5.** Explain and obtain consent. **6.** Connect the **oxygen tubing** to the cannula tubing. **7.** Keep the cannula from touching any surface. **8.** Turn on the oxygen supply (wall flowmeter or portable tank). **9.** Set the **correct liter flow**. **10.** Check that oxygen is flowing by feeling at the **cannula prongs**.',
'**11.** Hand hygiene and fresh gloves. **12.** With oxygen flowing, apply the cannula. **13a.** Place the **two prongs in the nostrils pointing down** (curving toward the face) and loop the tubing **around each ear**. **b.** Adjust the slide under the chin so the tips stay in place. **c.** Instruct the patient to breathe through the nose. **14.** Check the area for **sparks or flames**. **15–19.** Remove gloves, hand hygiene, **document** (device, flow, SpO₂, response), communicate and use standard precautions. Check the ears and nose for pressure sores during long-term use.',
order('Skill III sequence',['Review the order; gather equipment','Hand hygiene; identify with two identifiers; consent','Connect tubing; keep the cannula off surfaces','Turn on oxygen and set the ordered flow','Feel for flow at the prongs','Insert prongs pointing down; loop over ears','Adjust the slide; breathe through the nose','Check for sparks or flames; document'])]],
['Opening the airway',[
'In an unresponsive patient, the **tongue** is the most common cause of airway obstruction. The **head tilt–chin lift** opens the airway: one hand on the forehead tilts the head back while the fingers of the other hand lift the **mandible** forward. If a **spinal injury** is suspected, use the **jaw thrust** instead. **Airway adjuncts** keep it open: an **oropharyngeal airway (OPA)**—sized from the corner of the mouth to the earlobe or angle of the jaw—is used only in unconscious patients **without a gag reflex**; a **nasopharyngeal airway (NPA)**—sized from the nostril to the earlobe—can be used when a gag reflex is present (avoid with suspected skull fracture).',
'**Suctioning** clears secretions; limit each pass to about **10–15 seconds** and oxygenate before and after. Advanced airways include supraglottic devices and **endotracheal intubation**, with placement confirmed by **capnography**, breath sounds and chest X-ray.',
match('Airway tools',[['Head tilt–chin lift','Opens the airway with no spinal injury'],['Jaw thrust','Opens the airway with suspected spinal injury'],['OPA','Only when no gag reflex'],['NPA','Tolerated with a gag reflex'],['Suction pass','About 10–15 seconds'],['Capnography','Confirms tube placement by detecting CO₂']])]],
['Bag-valve-mask ventilation: Skill I',[
'Skill I has 18 steps in **4 minutes**. **1.** Gather equipment. **2.** Hand hygiene and gloves. **3.** Identify yourself. **4.** Assess **responsiveness** (judge: “Patient is not responding”). **5. Head tilt–chin lift:** hold the mandible with the middle and index fingers of one hand and lift the chin forward while tilting the head back with the other hand on the forehead. **6.** Check **breathing and pulse together** for **no more than 10 seconds** (judge: “Pulse is evident, no breathing noted”). **7.** Attach the **BVM to 100% oxygen** with the flowmeter. **8.** Position yourself at the **top of the patient’s head**.',
'**9.** Check that the mask and bag work. **10.** Seal the mask to the face with the **“C-E” hold**—thumb and index finger form a **C** pressing the mask down, and the other three fingers form an **E** lifting the jaw. **11.** Ventilate manually. **12.** Watch for **chest rise and fall**; if there is none, **reposition** the head and mask. **13.** Ventilate **every 5–6 seconds** (about 10–12 breaths per minute), each breath over about 1 second, until an airway adjunct is placed and chest rise is evident. **14–18.** Remove gloves, hand hygiene, document, communicate and use standard precautions. Avoid excessive ventilation, which inflates the stomach and lowers blood return to the heart.',
order('Skill I sequence',['Gloves; identify yourself; check responsiveness','Head tilt–chin lift','Check breathing and pulse together (≤10 seconds)','Attach BVM to 100% oxygen','Stand at the top of the head; check the device','C-E hold to seal the mask','Ventilate and watch for chest rise; reposition if none','One breath every 5–6 seconds until the adjunct is placed']),
flip('Skill I details',[['Breathing and pulse check','No more than 10 seconds'],['Oxygen','BVM attached to 100% O₂'],['Position','At the top of the patient’s head'],['C-E hold','C presses the mask; E lifts the jaw'],['Rate','One breath every 5–6 seconds'],['No chest rise','Reposition the head and mask']])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Oxygen and airway decisions',{a:{text:'A patient with COPD and chronic CO₂ retention has SpO₂ 85% on room air. The order says titrate to 88–92%. What device is a good choice? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['A Venturi mask or low-flow nasal cannula, titrated to 88–92%','b','Correct. Controlled oxygen to the target.',true],['A nonrebreather at 15 L/min','b','Too much oxygen may worsen CO₂ retention.',false]]},b:{text:'Later you find an unresponsive patient with a pulse but no breathing. After opening the airway, what do you do?',choices:[['Ventilate with a BVM on 100% oxygen, one breath every 5–6 seconds, watching chest rise','c','Correct. Rescue breathing with a pulse present.',true],['Start chest compressions immediately','c','With a pulse present, provide ventilations.',false]]},c:{text:'You maintain a C-E seal, call for help and continue until an airway adjunct is placed.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Oxygen is a drug: use the lowest concentration that reaches the target SpO₂ (often 92–96%, or 88–92% for CO₂ retainers); hazards include fire, toxicity, absorption atelectasis and ROP.','Cannula 1–6 L/min (24–44%), simple mask 5–10 (35–50%), nonrebreather 10–15 (60–90%), Venturi precise 24–50%, HFNC up to ~60 L/min; Skill III: order, identify, connect, set flow, check flow, prongs down and over the ears, check for flames, document.','Skill I: responsiveness, head tilt–chin lift, breathing and pulse ≤10 s, BVM on 100% O₂, C-E hold, watch chest rise, one breath every 5–6 seconds; jaw thrust for spinal injury; OPA only without gag reflex.'],terms:[['FiO₂','The fraction of inspired oxygen.'],['Bag-valve-mask','A hand-squeezed device used to ventilate a patient.'],['C-E hold','A hand position that seals the mask and lifts the jaw.']],mcqs:[q('What flow range is used for a nasal cannula?','1–6 L/min','10–15 L/min','15–25 L/min','0.1 L/min only','About 24–44% FiO₂.'),q('A nonrebreather mask should run at:','10–15 L/min','1–2 L/min','4 L/min','30 L/min','Bag stays one-third full.'),q('Which device delivers a precise FiO₂ for COPD?','Venturi mask','Simple mask','Nonrebreather','Nasal cannula','Air-entrainment.'),q('In Skill III, how are the prongs placed?','In the nostrils pointing down, tubing looped over the ears','Pointing up into the sinuses','In the mouth','Taped to the cheeks only','Then adjust the slide.'),q('How long may you check breathing and pulse in Skill I?','No more than 10 seconds','30 seconds','1 minute','5 seconds exactly','Then act.'),q('How often do you ventilate in Skill I?','Every 5–6 seconds','Every second','Every 15 seconds','Once a minute','Until an adjunct is placed.'),q('Which airway maneuver is used with suspected spinal injury?','Jaw thrust','Head tilt–chin lift','Neck hyperextension','Rolling the head side to side','Avoids neck movement.'),q('An OPA should be used only when:','The patient has no gag reflex','The patient is awake','The patient is talking','There is a skull fracture','Otherwise it causes vomiting.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would administer oxygen by nasal cannula following HOSA Skill III and how you would ventilate an unresponsive patient with a pulse following HOSA Skill I.',rubric:[['Cannula setup',['order','two identifiers','consent','connect','surface','flow','liter','check']],['Cannula application',['prongs','pointing down','ears','slide','nose','sparks','flames','document']],['Airway opening and assessment',['responsiveness','head tilt','chin lift','10 seconds','pulse','breathing']],['BVM ventilation',['100%','top of the head','c-e','seal','chest rise','reposition','5-6 seconds','adjunct']]],model:'For Skill III, I review the physician’s order, gather equipment, perform hand hygiene and put on gloves, greet the patient, identify them with two identifiers, explain the procedure and obtain consent. I connect the oxygen tubing to the cannula, keep the cannula from touching any surface, turn on the oxygen supply, set the ordered liter flow and feel at the prongs to confirm flow. After fresh hand hygiene and gloves, with oxygen flowing, I place the two prongs in the nostrils pointing down, loop the tubing over each ear, adjust the slide under the chin so the prongs stay in place and ask the patient to breathe through the nose. I check that there are no sparks or flames nearby, remove my gloves, perform hand hygiene and document the device, flow, SpO₂ and response. For Skill I, after gloving and identifying myself, I check responsiveness, open the airway with a head tilt–chin lift by lifting the mandible with two fingers while tilting the head back with my other hand on the forehead, and check breathing and pulse together for no more than 10 seconds. With a pulse but no breathing, I attach the bag-valve-mask to 100% oxygen, position myself at the top of the patient’s head, check the device, seal the mask with a C-E hold and ventilate, watching for chest rise and repositioning if there is none. I give one breath every 5–6 seconds until an airway adjunct is placed, then remove gloves, perform hand hygiene and document.'},sources:[RULES,NHLBI_O2,AHA_CPR,AARC]};

export const E9={title:'Aerosol medications, the hand-held nebulizer and airway clearance (Skill VII)',hook:'When a child wheezes in the emergency department, a respiratory therapist often delivers the first treatment—a mist of albuterol through a small nebulizer. Aerosol therapy puts medicine directly where it is needed: in the airways. Skill VII has 29 steps in 8 minutes, including monitoring for side effects and knowing when to stop.',objectives:['Describe aerosol delivery devices and how particle size affects deposition.','Identify common inhaled medications and their effects.','Give a hand-held nebulizer treatment following HOSA Skill VII.','Describe airway clearance and lung expansion therapies.'],sections:[
['Aerosol basics and devices',[
'An **aerosol** is a suspension of tiny particles in a gas. **Particle size** determines where medicine lands: particles of about **1–5 micrometers** reach the lower airways, larger particles deposit in the mouth and throat, and very small particles may be exhaled. Slow, deep breaths with a **breath-hold** improve deposition. Devices include **small-volume (hand-held) nebulizers**, powered by compressed gas or a compressor at about **6–8 L/min**; **metered-dose inhalers (MDIs)**, best used with a **spacer** or valved holding chamber; **dry powder inhalers (DPIs)**, which require a fast, deep inhalation; and **soft mist inhalers**.',
'Nebulizers need little coordination and work well for young children, very breathless patients and high doses, but they take longer and require cleaning. MDIs with spacers are as effective as nebulizers for most patients when used correctly and are faster and cheaper. Teaching **correct technique** is a key RT role.',
match('Device and key point',[['Small-volume nebulizer','Little coordination needed; takes 5–15 minutes'],['MDI with spacer','Press once, inhale slowly, hold breath'],['Dry powder inhaler','Needs a fast, deep breath'],['1–5 micrometer particles','Reach the lower airways'],['Breath-hold','Improves deposition'],['Mask nebulizer','Useful for young children']])]],
['Inhaled medications',[
'**Short-acting beta₂ agonists** (albuterol, levalbuterol) relax airway smooth muscle within minutes; side effects include **tachycardia**, **tremor**, nervousness and low potassium. **Short-acting anticholinergics** (ipratropium) also open airways, especially in COPD. **Long-acting** bronchodilators (LABA: salmeterol, formoterol; LAMA: tiotropium) are maintenance drugs. **Inhaled corticosteroids** (budesonide, fluticasone) reduce inflammation over time; patients **rinse the mouth** to prevent thrush. **Mucolytics** such as **dornase alfa** and **hypertonic saline** thin secretions (hypertonic saline can trigger bronchospasm, so a bronchodilator is often given first).',
'Other inhaled therapies include **racemic epinephrine** for croup and post-extubation stridor, inhaled antibiotics (tobramycin) for cystic fibrosis and **inhaled pulmonary vasodilators**. A typical albuterol unit dose is **2.5 mg in 3 mL** of saline. Always verify the **order**, the drug, dose, route and patient before giving any medication.',
sort('Bronchodilator, anti-inflammatory or mucolytic?',['Bronchodilator','Anti-inflammatory','Mucolytic'],[['Albuterol',0],['Budesonide',1],['Dornase alfa',2],['Ipratropium',0],['Fluticasone',1],['Hypertonic saline',2]])]],
['Before the treatment: Skill VII steps 1–13',[
'**1.** Obtain and review the **physician’s order**. **2.** Obtain equipment. **3.** Obtain the medication as ordered. **4.** **Knock and provide privacy**. **5.** Hand hygiene and gloves. **6.** Greet and identify the patient with **two identifiers**. **7.** **Interview** the patient: home nebulizer use and home oxygen use. **8.** Explain and obtain consent. **9.** **Baseline assessment**: **pulse**, **breath sounds** and **respiratory rate**. **10–11.** Remove gloves, hand hygiene and fresh gloves.',
'**12.** Assemble and **test** the equipment before applying it. **13. Prepare the medication**: accurately prepare the prescribed dose and **aseptically** add it to the nebulizer cup. Baseline measurements matter because you will compare them after the treatment to judge whether it helped and whether side effects occurred.',
order('Before the nebulizer',['Review the order; get equipment and medication','Knock; privacy; hand hygiene; gloves','Identify with two identifiers','Ask about home nebulizer and oxygen use','Explain; obtain consent','Baseline pulse, breath sounds and respiratory rate','Assemble and test equipment','Prepare the medication aseptically'])]],
['During and after: Skill VII steps 14–29',[
'**14.** Verbalize activating gas flow to the **manufacturer’s required flow** (often 6–8 L/min) or as limited by the gas source. **15.** Select a **mouthpiece or mask** and connect it. **16.** **Observe for aerosol mist** before giving it to the patient. **17.** Hand the nebulizer to the patient or place the mask. **18.** Coach the patient to **breathe slowly through the mouth** and, after **5–6 regular breaths**, **hold the breath for 10 seconds**. **19.** **Monitor** for adverse responses: **stop** if the **heart rate increases more than 30 beats per minute** or the **respiratory rate rises above 22**.',
'**20.** Continue until the nebulizer begins to **sputter**. **21.** **Remove the nebulizer or mask before turning off the gas**. **22.** Turn off the gas and **encourage the patient to cough**. **23.** Ask how the patient feels. **24.** **Post-assessment**: breath sounds, respiratory rate and pulse. **25.** Disassemble and **rinse the nebulizer with sterile water**, then air-dry (or discard if soiled). **26.** Remove gloves and hand hygiene. **27.** **Document** the procedure and response. **28–29.** Communicate and use standard precautions.',
order('During and after',['Set gas flow per the manufacturer','Connect mouthpiece or mask; check for mist','Coach slow mouth breathing with a 10-second hold every 5–6 breaths','Monitor: stop if HR rises >30 or RR >22','Continue until sputtering','Remove the device before turning off gas','Encourage coughing; ask how the patient feels','Post-assess; rinse and air-dry; document']),
{t:'example',title:'Should you stop?',prompt:'Baseline HR is 88. Five minutes into an albuterol treatment, HR is 124 and RR is 20. (Values invented for practice.)',steps:['HR increase: 124 − 88 = 36 beats per minute.','The rating sheet says stop if HR increases more than 30.','RR 20 is not above 22.'],answer:'Stop the treatment, stay with the patient and notify the physician or nurse.'}]],
['Airway clearance and lung expansion',[
'**Airway clearance** helps patients with retained secretions—cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, neuromuscular weakness, post-operative patients. Techniques include **directed coughing** and the **huff cough**, the **active cycle of breathing technique**, **chest physiotherapy** (postural drainage with percussion and vibration), **positive expiratory pressure (PEP)** and **oscillating PEP** devices (flutter valves), **high-frequency chest wall oscillation vests**, **mechanical insufflation-exsufflation** (“cough assist”) for weak patients and **suctioning** when patients cannot clear secretions.',
'**Lung expansion therapy** prevents and treats **atelectasis**, especially after chest and abdominal surgery. **Incentive spirometry** encourages slow, deep breaths with a 3–5 second hold, about **10 times an hour** while awake. **Early mobility**, deep breathing, **CPAP** and **intermittent positive pressure breathing** help some patients. Signs of atelectasis include diminished breath sounds, fine crackles, fever and low SpO₂.',
"Document each therapy’s indication, technique and the patient’s response.",
match('Therapy and purpose',[['Incentive spirometry','Prevents post-operative atelectasis'],['Huff cough','Moves secretions with an open throat'],['Flutter (oscillating PEP)','Vibrates airways to loosen mucus'],['Chest wall oscillation vest','Airway clearance for cystic fibrosis'],['Cough assist device','Helps weak patients cough'],['Postural drainage','Gravity drains lung segments']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Nebulizer treatment',{a:{text:'An 8-year-old with wheezing needs albuterol by nebulizer. Before starting, what baseline data do you collect? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Pulse, breath sounds and respiratory rate','b','Correct. You compare these afterward.',true],['Only the temperature','b','Skill VII requires pulse, breath sounds and RR.',false]]},b:{text:'The treatment begins to sputter. What do you do next?',choices:[['Remove the mask, then turn off the gas, encourage coughing and reassess','c','Correct. Device off the patient before the gas.',true],['Turn off the gas while the mask is still on','c','Remove the device first.',false]]},c:{text:'Post-treatment, wheezes are decreased and RR has fallen. You rinse the nebulizer with sterile water and document the improvement.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Aerosol particles of 1–5 µm reach the lower airways; nebulizers (6–8 L/min), MDIs with spacers and DPIs each need correct technique; slow breathing and breath-holds improve deposition.','Albuterol and ipratropium bronchodilate (watch HR and tremor); inhaled steroids reduce inflammation (rinse mouth); dornase alfa and hypertonic saline thin mucus.','Skill VII: order, privacy, two identifiers, history, baseline pulse, breath sounds and RR, test and prepare aseptically, mist check, coach a 10-second hold, stop if HR rises >30 or RR >22, run to sputter, remove the device before the gas, cough, reassess, rinse with sterile water, document.'],terms:[['Aerosol','A suspension of fine particles in a gas.'],['Spacer','A chamber attached to an MDI that slows and holds the medicine.'],['Atelectasis','Collapse of part of the lung.']],mcqs:[q('Which particle size best reaches the lower airways?','1–5 micrometers','50 micrometers','100 micrometers','0.01 micrometers','Smaller particles may be exhaled.'),q('A common albuterol unit dose for nebulization is:','2.5 mg in 3 mL','250 mg in 1 mL','25 mg in 30 mL','0.025 mg','Standard unit dose.'),q('In Skill VII, stop the treatment if the heart rate rises by more than:','30 beats per minute','5 beats per minute','10 beats per minute','60 beats per minute','Or RR above 22.'),q('How long is the breath-hold coached in Skill VII?','10 seconds','1 second','30 seconds','No breath-hold','After 5–6 regular breaths.'),q('When is the nebulizer treatment finished?','When it begins to sputter','After exactly 1 minute','When the patient asks','When the cup is still full','Medicine is used up.'),q('What do you do before turning off the gas?','Remove the nebulizer or mask from the patient','Turn off the lights','Wash the nebulizer','Disconnect the oxygen wall unit','Per the rating sheet.'),q('Patients should rinse their mouth after:','Inhaled corticosteroids','Albuterol','Ipratropium','Saline','Prevents thrush.'),q('Incentive spirometry mainly prevents:','Atelectasis','Asthma','Pneumothorax','Lung cancer','After surgery.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would give a hand-held nebulizer treatment following HOSA Skill VII, including assessment before and after, coaching, monitoring and cleaning.',rubric:[['Preparation',['order','medication','privacy','two identifiers','home nebulizer','oxygen','consent']],['Baseline and setup',['pulse','breath sounds','respiratory rate','test','aseptically','flow','mist']],['Coaching and monitoring',['slowly','mouth','5-6','10 seconds','heart rate','30','22','stop']],['Finish',['sputter','remove','before','gas','cough','post','sterile water','document']]],model:'I review the physician’s order, gather equipment and obtain the ordered medication, knock and provide privacy, perform hand hygiene and put on gloves, then greet the patient and identify them with two identifiers. I ask about home nebulizer and home oxygen use, explain the procedure and obtain consent. For a baseline, I measure the pulse, listen to breath sounds and count the respiratory rate. After changing gloves, I assemble and test the nebulizer, accurately prepare the prescribed dose and add it to the cup aseptically. I set the gas flow to the manufacturer’s requirement, connect a mouthpiece or mask and check for mist before giving it to the patient. I coach the patient to breathe slowly through the mouth and, after five or six regular breaths, hold the breath for 10 seconds. Throughout, I monitor for adverse effects and stop if the heart rate increases by more than 30 beats per minute or the respiratory rate rises above 22. When the nebulizer sputters, I remove it from the patient before turning off the gas, encourage the patient to cough and ask how they feel. I reassess breath sounds, respiratory rate and pulse to judge the response, rinse the nebulizer with sterile water and let it air-dry, remove my gloves, perform hand hygiene and document the treatment and the patient’s response.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_NEB,MEDLINE_MDI,NHLBI_CF]};

export const E10={title:'Newborn assessment, pediatric respiratory care and patient education (Skill V)',hook:'The first minute after birth is one of the most important in a person’s life. Most newborns start breathing on their own, but about 1 in 10 needs some help, and respiratory therapists are often part of the delivery-room team. Later in life, education and long-term care help people with chronic lung disease stay out of the hospital. Patient education and long-term care are 10% of the test.',objectives:['Assess and support a newborn after delivery following HOSA Skill V and assign an Apgar score.','Describe common neonatal and pediatric respiratory disorders.','Teach inhaler use, action plans, home oxygen and smoking cessation.','Explain pulmonary rehabilitation and long-term care.'],sections:[
['Newborn transition and the Apgar score',[
'At birth, fluid leaves the lungs, the baby begins breathing, lung blood vessels open and circulation changes from the fetal pattern. Initial steps (from the Neonatal Resuscitation Program) are to **warm, dry, stimulate**, **position the airway** and **clear secretions if needed**, then assess **breathing** and **heart rate**. A heart rate **above 100** with good breathing is reassuring; a heart rate **below 100** or apnea calls for **positive-pressure ventilation**.',
'The **Apgar score**, assessed at **1 and 5 minutes** after birth, rates five signs from 0 to 2: **Appearance** (skin color), **Pulse** (heart rate: absent 0, below 100 = 1, 100 or above = 2), **Grimace** (reflex response), **Activity** (muscle tone) and **Respiration**. A score of **7–10** is reassuring, **4–6** moderately abnormal and **0–3** low. The score guides ongoing care but is not used to decide whether to begin resuscitation.',
{t:'table',caption:'Apgar scoring',head:['Sign','0','1','2'],rows:[['Appearance','Blue or pale all over','Body pink, hands and feet blue','Pink all over'],['Pulse','Absent','Below 100','100 or above'],['Grimace','No response','Grimace','Cry, cough or sneeze'],['Activity','Limp','Some flexion','Active motion'],['Respiration','Absent','Weak, irregular','Strong cry']]},
chk('A newborn at 1 minute is pink with blue hands, heart rate 110, grimaces, has some flexion and a strong cry. What is the Apgar score?',['7','10','6','5'],'Appearance 1 + Pulse 2 + Grimace 1 + Activity 1 + Respiration 2 = 7.')]],
['Assessment of the newborn: Skill V',[
'Skill V has 23 steps in **5 minutes**. **1.** Obtain equipment. **2.** Hand hygiene. **3.** Gloves. **4.** Identify yourself to the care team, mother and family. **5.** Drape a **warm, dry blanket** over your arms to receive the infant. **6.** **Hug the infant to your chest** to prevent dropping. **7.** Place the infant **supine in the warmer**. **8.** Position the **head closest to you** in a **neutral or slightly extended (“sniffing”)** position. **9.** **Dry** with the blanket, providing **tactile stimulation**. **10.** As the blanket becomes wet, **remove it** and use a dry blanket, continuing stimulation for **one minute**.',
'**11.** Inspect the chest and note the breathing pattern. If there is **increased work of breathing**, **12.** use a **bulb syringe**: squeeze the bulb **before** entering, suction the **mouth first**, release and clean, then each **nare** (“**mouth before nose**,” because suctioning the nose first can make the baby gasp and inhale secretions). **13.** Use a stethoscope for **breath sounds and heart rate** (judge: “heart rate greater than 100, respirations adequate”). **14.** Assess **muscle tone, response to stimuli and skin color**. **15.** Verbalize the **Apgar at 1 and 5 minutes** (judge: “The infant is stable”). **16.** Wrap the infant in a dry towel and place a **hat**. **17.** Ensure safety and comfort. **18.** Verbalize handing the infant to the appropriate personnel or mother. **19–23.** Remove gloves, hand hygiene, document, communicate and use standard precautions.',
order('Skill V sequence',['Hand hygiene and gloves; introduce yourself','Receive the infant in a warm, dry blanket; hug to chest','Place supine in the warmer, head toward you in sniffing position','Dry and stimulate; replace the wet blanket; continue 1 minute','Inspect breathing; bulb-suction mouth then nose if work of breathing is increased','Listen to breath sounds and heart rate','Assess tone, response and color; Apgar at 1 and 5 minutes','Wrap, add a hat and hand the infant to the mother or staff']),
flip('Skill V reminders',[['Receiving','Warm, dry blanket; hug to chest'],['Position','Supine, head toward you, sniffing position'],['Stimulation','Dry and rub for one minute'],['Suction order','Mouth before nose'],['Bulb syringe','Squeeze before entering'],['Heat loss','Dry towel and a hat']])]],
['Neonatal and pediatric respiratory disorders',[
'**Transient tachypnea of the newborn (TTN)**—the **most common** newborn respiratory disorder—comes from delayed clearance of lung fluid, especially after cesarean birth; it causes fast breathing that usually resolves within **1–3 days** with supportive care. **Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS)** affects **premature** infants who lack **surfactant**, causing grunting, retractions, nasal flaring and a “ground-glass” X-ray; treatment includes **CPAP** and **surfactant replacement**, and steroids given to the mother before preterm birth help prevent it. **Meconium aspiration** can cause airway obstruction and pneumonitis. **Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD)** is chronic lung disease in premature infants who needed oxygen or ventilation. **Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN)** keeps lung blood pressure high.',
'In older infants and children: **bronchiolitis**, usually from **RSV**, causes wheezing, crackles and feeding trouble—supportive care with suctioning, oxygen and hydration; **croup** (laryngotracheobronchitis, often parainfluenza virus) causes a **barking cough** and inspiratory **stridor**, treated with steroids and **racemic epinephrine**; **epiglottitis** (now rare thanks to the **Hib** vaccine) causes high fever, **drooling** and the **tripod** position—a true emergency in which the throat should not be examined until the airway is secured. **Foreign-body aspiration** usually lodges in the right bronchus.',
match('Neonatal and pediatric disorders',[['Most common newborn respiratory disorder','Transient tachypnea of the newborn'],['Surfactant deficiency in preemies','Respiratory distress syndrome'],['Chronic lung disease after prematurity','Bronchopulmonary dysplasia'],['RSV in infants','Bronchiolitis'],['Barking cough and stridor','Croup'],['Drooling, tripod position, high fever','Epiglottitis']])]],
['Patient education',[
'Education prevents exacerbations and readmissions. Teach **inhaler technique** (MDI with spacer: shake, exhale, seal lips, press once, inhale slowly, hold for about 10 seconds; DPI: fast, deep breath), using **teach-back** to confirm. Provide written **asthma or COPD action plans** with green (doing well), yellow (getting worse—use rescue medicine and call) and red (emergency) zones, including **peak flow** targets for asthma. Explain **trigger avoidance**, vaccines (influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19, RSV for eligible adults) and when to seek help.',
'**Smoking cessation** is the single most important step for many patients: use the **5 A’s** (Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange), offer **nicotine replacement** and medicines such as **varenicline**, and refer to **1-800-QUIT-NOW**. For **home oxygen**, teach safe use and storage, never smoking, the ordered flow, travel planning and equipment cleaning. Tailor education to health literacy, culture and language, and involve family caregivers.',
order('MDI with spacer technique',['Shake the inhaler and attach the spacer','Breathe out fully','Seal lips around the mouthpiece','Press the inhaler once','Breathe in slowly and deeply','Hold the breath about 10 seconds','Wait before a second puff if ordered'])]],
['Pulmonary rehabilitation and long-term care',[
'**Pulmonary rehabilitation** is a supervised program of **exercise training**, **breathing techniques** (pursed-lip and diaphragmatic breathing), **education**, nutrition counseling and psychological support for people with chronic lung disease. It improves breathlessness, exercise capacity and quality of life and reduces hospital readmissions for COPD; outcomes are often measured with the **6-minute walk test**. **Energy conservation** techniques—planning, pacing, sitting for tasks and coordinating breathing with movement—help daily activities.',
'Long-term care settings include **home care**, skilled nursing facilities and **long-term acute care hospitals** for patients on prolonged mechanical ventilation, including those with **tracheostomies**. RTs manage home ventilators and CPAP, teach caregivers suctioning and trach care, and support **advance care planning** and **palliative care** so treatment matches patients’ goals.',
sort('Part of pulmonary rehab?',['Core component','Not a core component'],[['Supervised exercise training',0],['Pursed-lip breathing instruction',0],['Education on medications and action plans',0],['Strict bed rest',1],['Nutrition counseling',0],['Avoiding all physical activity',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Delivery room',{a:{text:'A term baby is born and placed in the warmer. After drying, the baby has grunting and retractions with secretions in the mouth. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Suction with a bulb syringe—mouth first, then nose—then reassess','b','Correct. Clear the airway when work of breathing is increased.',true],['Suction the nose first','b','Mouth before nose to avoid aspiration.',false]]},b:{text:'You hear a heart rate above 100 and adequate respirations. What is next?',choices:[['Assess tone, response and color and verbalize the Apgar at 1 and 5 minutes','c','Correct. Then wrap the baby and add a hat.',true],['Start chest compressions','c','A heart rate over 100 does not need compressions.',false]]},c:{text:'The infant is stable. You wrap the baby in a dry towel, place a hat, hand the infant to the mother and document.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Newborn: warm, dry, stimulate, position and clear the airway; HR >100 is reassuring; Apgar (appearance, pulse, grimace, activity, respiration) at 1 and 5 minutes, 7–10 reassuring.','Skill V: warm blanket, hug to chest, supine in the warmer with head toward you, dry and stimulate 1 minute, bulb suction mouth then nose if needed, listen to HR and breath sounds, Apgar, towel and hat; TTN is the most common newborn respiratory disorder.','Teach inhaler technique, action plans, vaccines, smoking cessation (5 A’s, 1-800-QUIT-NOW) and home oxygen safety; pulmonary rehab combines exercise, breathing techniques and education.'],terms:[['Apgar score','A 0–10 rating of a newborn at 1 and 5 minutes after birth.'],['Transient tachypnea of the newborn','Fast breathing from retained lung fluid that resolves in days.'],['Pulmonary rehabilitation','Supervised exercise and education for chronic lung disease.']],mcqs:[q('What is the most common respiratory disorder in newborns?','Transient tachypnea of the newborn','Meconium aspiration syndrome','Bronchopulmonary dysplasia','Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn','HOSA sample question.'),q('When is the Apgar score assessed?','At 1 and 5 minutes','At birth only','At 24 hours','At 10 and 30 minutes','Repeated if low.'),q('A newborn heart rate of 90 earns how many Apgar points for pulse?','1','0','2','3','Below 100 = 1.'),q('With a bulb syringe, which do you suction first?','Mouth','Nose','Both at once','Ears','Mouth before nose.'),q('How long do you dry and stimulate in Skill V?','One minute','Ten seconds','Five minutes','No stimulation','Replace wet blankets.'),q('Respiratory distress syndrome in preemies is caused by a lack of:','Surfactant','Hemoglobin','Platelets','Insulin','Treated with surfactant and CPAP.'),q('A barking cough with inspiratory stridor in a toddler suggests:','Croup','Epiglottitis','Bronchiolitis','Asthma','Steroids and racemic epinephrine.'),q('Which number connects people to free quit-smoking help in the U.S.?','1-800-QUIT-NOW','911','988','411','State quitlines.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would receive and assess a newborn following HOSA Skill V, including when to use a bulb syringe and how to assign an Apgar score.',rubric:[['Preparation and receiving',['hand hygiene','gloves','identify','warm','dry blanket','hug','chest']],['Positioning and stimulation',['warmer','supine','head','neutral','sniffing','dry','stimulation','one minute']],['Airway and assessment',['work of breathing','bulb syringe','squeeze','mouth','nose','stethoscope','heart rate','100','tone','color']],['Apgar and finish',['apgar','1 and 5','appearance','pulse','grimace','activity','respiration','hat','mother','document']]],model:'I gather equipment, perform hand hygiene, put on gloves and introduce myself to the care team, the mother and her family. I drape a warm, dry blanket over my arms to receive the infant and hug the baby to my chest so I do not drop them, then place the infant supine in the warmer with the head closest to me in a neutral or slightly extended sniffing position. I dry the baby with the blanket, which also provides tactile stimulation, replace the wet blanket with a dry one and continue stimulating for one minute. I inspect the chest and breathing pattern; if there is increased work of breathing, such as grunting or retractions with secretions, I squeeze the bulb syringe before inserting it, suction the mouth first and then each nostril, cleaning the bulb between passes. I listen with a stethoscope to breath sounds and heart rate—a heart rate above 100 with adequate breathing is reassuring—and assess muscle tone, response to stimulation and skin color. I verbalize the Apgar score at 1 and 5 minutes, giving 0–2 points each for appearance, pulse, grimace, activity and respiration, where 7–10 is reassuring. When the infant is stable, I wrap the baby in a dry towel with a hat to prevent heat loss, ensure safety, hand the infant to the mother or appropriate staff, remove my gloves, perform hand hygiene and document.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_APGAR,MEDLINE_TTN,MEDLINE_RDS,ALA_REHAB,CDC_QUIT]};
